<!doctype html>
<html lang="nl">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
    <!--
      The language on the <html> element above is the app's default locale: the first one
      `frontend/i18n.json` declares, which is what the app opens in. It is what a screen reader
      and a crawler meet before the bundle runs; from mount onwards LocaleProvider keeps it on
      whichever locale is active, so a reader who picks another language is announced in that
      one. Put a different locale first in i18n.json and change this attribute with it.
    -->
    <!--
      The tab mark. `public/favicon.svg` is served at the site root by Vite and copied into the
      production build as-is, so replacing that one file is the whole change — no import, no
      hashed asset name, nothing here to edit.

      Declared rather than left to the browser's default request for /favicon.ico: this is a
      single-page app, so an unrouted request does not 404, it returns index.html, and the tab
      shows a generic globe with no sign that anything went wrong.

      The file carries both appearances in its own `prefers-color-scheme` block, because a
      favicon is a separate document that never sees this page's tokens.
    -->
    <link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href="/favicon.svg" />
    <!--
      The palette, before the first paint. The theme is a person's own choice, so it lives in
      localStorage and only script can read it — and the app's own bundle cannot do it in
      time: the body is empty until React commits, so the browser has already painted a light
      canvas by then and a viewer who chose a dark palette sees a white flash on every reload.
      This script is deliberately BLOCKING and served from public/ verbatim; the file itself
      records why each of those is load-bearing. An app that ships on a named palette declares
      it in BOTH places rather than editing the script: `defaultTheme` in layout-contract.json,
      which is what the app applies, and the same name as `data-theme` on the <html> element
      above, which is what paints it before the bundle exists. The attribute on its own would
      be removed on mount, because the provider's default is to follow the platform.
    -->
    <script src="/theme-bootstrap.js"></script>
    <title>{{ project_name }}</title>
  </head>
  <body>
    <div id="root"></div>
    <script type="module" src="/src/main.tsx"></script>
  </body>
</html>
